{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Horizon-dependent bias: forecasts are slightly high at short horizons (≈D+1 to D+10, typically +0.2 to +1.0) and increasingly low at longer horizons during rallies (≈D+20 to D+30, often -2 to -8). Apply a bias curve that transitions from small negative adjustment early to positive adjustment late when trend is up.",
    "Trend underreaction / lag: when spot breaks upward (e.g., move into low/mid-70s), the model path rises too slowly and tops out too low. Add a trend-following boost to D+15 to D+30 proportional to recent 5–10d momentum.",
    "Overshoot on local peaks: around short-term highs the model tends to project levels that are too elevated and too persistent (insufficient near-term pullback). After a sharp up-day, dampen D+1 to D+7 by a fraction of the last 1–3 day move (mean-reversion kicker).",
    "Amplitude compression: the model’s forecast range is too narrow—underestimates upside tails more than downside tails. Increase volatility/scenario width, especially beyond D+15, by scaling deviations from the 30d mean upward when momentum is positive.",
    "Regime switch heuristic: if last price > 30d mean + 1σ (strong up-regime), shift the whole forecast curve up and steepen the back end; if last price < 30d mean - 1σ, do the opposite (reduce early-horizon optimism and allow deeper near-term downside)."
  ]
}
